WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.195
Lesson 4.3. Liquid staking (LSTs).

00:00:02.195 --> 00:00:06.103
By the end, you'll know exactly how these tokens

00:00:06.103 --> 00:00:09.452
accrue value, and how they can trade at a

00:00:09.452 --> 00:00:13.280
discount, before you ever use one as collateral.

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:15.870
Here's the plan.

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:19.577
What an L.S.T. actually represents, and the two different

00:00:19.577 --> 00:00:22.625
ways it can pass staking rewards on to you.

00:00:22.625 --> 00:00:24.936
How a discount can appear under stress,

00:00:24.936 --> 00:00:27.200
and exactly who it actually hurts.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.526
A full worked example, using the source

00:00:29.526 --> 00:00:32.385
material's own accrual and discount numbers.

00:00:32.385 --> 00:00:34.230
And a checklist for using

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:37.140
L.S.T.s safely, especially as collateral.

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:40.505
What an LST actually represents.

00:00:41.805 --> 00:00:43.869
Here's exactly what you're holding when

00:00:43.869 --> 00:00:45.820
you hold a liquid staking token.

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:49.252
It represents your original staked assets, plus every

00:00:49.252 --> 00:00:52.430
reward that's accrued on them since, bundled into

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:55.353
a single token you can still trade, transfer,

00:00:55.353 --> 00:00:58.149
or use as collateral elsewhere in DeFi, all

00:00:58.149 --> 00:01:01.500
without ever unstaking your underlying position.

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:04.052
That's the entire point of the design: keeping

00:01:04.052 --> 00:01:06.604
your capital liquid and useful, while it keeps

00:01:06.604 --> 00:01:08.885
earning staking rewards in the background.

00:01:10.185 --> 00:01:13.053
Here's the two different ways an L.S.T. can

00:01:13.053 --> 00:01:16.117
actually pass those accrued rewards on to you,

00:01:16.117 --> 00:01:19.090
since protocols genuinely differ on this.

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:23.038
A rebasing L.S.T. grows your token balance directly, over

00:01:23.038 --> 00:01:26.306
time, so you hold more tokens tomorrow than you

00:01:26.306 --> 00:01:29.465
do today, each still worth about the same.

00:01:29.465 --> 00:01:33.910
An exchange-rate L.S.T. instead keeps your token balance fixed,

00:01:33.910 --> 00:01:37.731
but each token becomes redeemable for a growing amount

00:01:37.731 --> 00:01:41.690
of the underlying asset, as rewards accrue underneath it.

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:46.979
Worth being precise about why this distinction actually matters

00:01:46.979 --> 00:01:50.395
in practice, beyond just being a technical detail.

00:01:50.395 --> 00:01:54.121
A rebasing token can complicate certain integrations, since

00:01:54.121 --> 00:01:57.102
some contracts don't expect a balance to change

00:01:57.102 --> 00:01:59.265
on its own without a transfer.

00:01:59.265 --> 00:02:03.113
An exchange-rate token avoids that specific problem, which is

00:02:03.113 --> 00:02:06.093
part of why it's become the more common design,

00:02:06.093 --> 00:02:08.886
but it does mean the token's price, not your

00:02:08.886 --> 00:02:11.555
balance, is what carries the growing value.

00:02:12.855 --> 00:02:15.030
That 3% a year, compounded.

00:02:16.330 --> 00:02:19.822
Here's that same three percent annual rate, extended out

00:02:19.822 --> 00:02:22.702
across five years, so you can see what genuine

00:02:22.702 --> 00:02:26.065
compounding actually does to the redemption value.

00:02:26.065 --> 00:02:28.981
Starting at one point zero zero zero, year

00:02:28.981 --> 00:02:31.655
one closes at one point zero three.

00:02:31.655 --> 00:02:34.495
By year three, one point zero nine three.

00:02:34.495 --> 00:02:37.015
By year five, one point one five nine,

00:02:37.015 --> 00:02:40.311
meaning each token then redeems for nearly sixteen

00:02:40.311 --> 00:02:43.348
percent more of the underlying than it started

00:02:43.348 --> 00:02:47.354
with, entirely from staking rewards compounding on themselves.

00:02:48.654 --> 00:02:49.744
Quick check.

00:02:49.744 --> 00:02:51.791
What are the two ways an L.S.T.

00:02:51.791 --> 00:02:53.519
can pass rewards on to you?

00:02:57.939 --> 00:03:01.484
The answer: rebasing, where your token balance itself

00:03:01.484 --> 00:03:04.438
grows, or a rising exchange rate, where each

00:03:04.438 --> 00:03:07.129
token redeems for more of the underlying.

00:03:08.429 --> 00:03:10.174
How a discount actually appears.

00:03:11.474 --> 00:03:14.369
Here's exactly how this gap opens up, since

00:03:14.369 --> 00:03:17.658
an L.S.T.'s market price and its redemption value

00:03:17.658 --> 00:03:20.919
aren't actually the same thing, mechanically.

00:03:20.919 --> 00:03:23.466
Redemption value is what the L.S.T. is

00:03:23.466 --> 00:03:26.535
genuinely worth, based on its accrued rewards,

00:03:26.535 --> 00:03:29.016
if you're willing to wait through the

00:03:29.016 --> 00:03:32.124
protocol's own unstaking queue to claim it.

00:03:32.124 --> 00:03:35.349
Market price is simply whatever someone else will pay

00:03:35.349 --> 00:03:37.919
for it, right now, on the open market.

00:03:37.919 --> 00:03:39.985
In calm conditions, arbitrage keeps

00:03:39.985 --> 00:03:42.179
these two numbers close together.

00:03:42.179 --> 00:03:44.574
Under real stress, that arbitrage can

00:03:44.574 --> 00:03:46.339
break down, and a gap opens.

00:03:47.639 --> 00:03:50.256
Worth being precise about who actually has the

00:03:50.256 --> 00:03:52.839
power to close this gap, and who doesn't.

00:03:52.839 --> 00:03:55.297
Anyone willing, and able, to simply wait

00:03:55.297 --> 00:03:58.294
through the redemption queue can eventually claim

00:03:58.294 --> 00:04:01.291
the full redemption value, regardless of whatever

00:04:01.291 --> 00:04:03.869
the market price does in the meantime.

00:04:03.869 --> 00:04:06.836
The discount only becomes a real, permanent loss

00:04:06.836 --> 00:04:09.197
for whoever is forced to sell into the

00:04:09.197 --> 00:04:12.224
market immediately, unable to wait that queue out.

00:04:13.524 --> 00:04:17.255
Here's a distinction worth keeping clear, since an L.S.T.

00:04:17.255 --> 00:04:20.922
discount and a genuine protocol failure can look similar

00:04:20.922 --> 00:04:24.889
from a chart alone, but mean completely different things.

00:04:24.889 --> 00:04:27.197
A discount is a temporary market gap,

00:04:27.197 --> 00:04:29.627
fully backed the entire time, and fully

00:04:29.627 --> 00:04:32.599
recoverable simply by waiting out the queue.

00:04:32.599 --> 00:04:35.703
A genuine depeg, from an actual protocol failure,

00:04:35.703 --> 00:04:38.806
means the backing itself is impaired, and waiting

00:04:38.806 --> 00:04:41.289
doesn't necessarily fix anything at all.

00:04:42.589 --> 00:04:43.679
Quick check.

00:04:43.679 --> 00:04:46.264
What exactly is an L.S.T. discount?

00:04:50.684 --> 00:04:53.204
The answer: its market price falling below

00:04:53.204 --> 00:04:55.723
what it would actually redeem for, through

00:04:55.723 --> 00:04:57.774
the protocol's own unstaking queue.

00:04:59.074 --> 00:05:00.079
Worked example.

00:05:01.379 --> 00:05:04.373
Here's the accrual side first, over an ordinary

00:05:04.373 --> 00:05:06.919
year, with no panic involved at all.

00:05:06.919 --> 00:05:10.722
An exchange-rate L.S.T. starts the year redeemable

00:05:10.722 --> 00:05:13.108
for one point zero zero zero of

00:05:13.108 --> 00:05:15.794
the underlying asset, per token.

00:05:15.794 --> 00:05:18.994
Over that year, staking rewards accrue underneath it.

00:05:18.994 --> 00:05:21.688
By year's end, it's redeemable for one

00:05:21.688 --> 00:05:24.382
point zero three zero, a genuine three

00:05:24.382 --> 00:05:27.421
percent gain, entirely from staking rewards.

00:05:28.721 --> 00:05:31.777
Now here's what happens when a panic hits, right

00:05:31.777 --> 00:05:34.896
at that same one point zero three zero redemption

00:05:34.896 --> 00:05:38.626
value, and the market and redemption value split apart.

00:05:38.626 --> 00:05:42.748
Patient holders, willing to enter the protocol's own redemption

00:05:42.748 --> 00:05:45.968
queue and simply wait, still recover the full one

00:05:45.968 --> 00:05:49.166
point zero three zero they're genuinely owed.

00:05:49.166 --> 00:05:52.134
Forced sellers, who need to exit immediately on

00:05:52.134 --> 00:05:54.545
the open market, instead sell at a two

00:05:54.545 --> 00:05:57.451
percent discount to that same redemption value.

00:05:58.751 --> 00:06:01.428
Here's the one sentence this entire worked

00:06:01.428 --> 00:06:04.167
example exists to prove, worth holding onto

00:06:04.167 --> 00:06:07.206
more than the specific percentages involved.

00:06:07.206 --> 00:06:09.907
The exact same token, worth the exact same

00:06:09.907 --> 00:06:13.111
one point zero three zero underneath, delivers two

00:06:13.111 --> 00:06:16.817
completely different outcomes, purely based on whether its

00:06:16.817 --> 00:06:18.876
holder could afford to wait.

00:06:18.876 --> 00:06:21.043
That gap is never about the asset's

00:06:21.043 --> 00:06:23.451
real value; it's entirely about who was

00:06:23.451 --> 00:06:24.956
forced to sell, and when.

00:06:26.256 --> 00:06:27.346
Quick check.

00:06:27.346 --> 00:06:30.481
Who's actually hurt most by an L.S.T. discount?

00:06:34.901 --> 00:06:37.412
The answer: forced sellers, who can't wait

00:06:37.412 --> 00:06:40.216
out the redemption queue, and leveraged holders

00:06:40.216 --> 00:06:42.961
who get liquidated directly into that discount.

00:06:44.261 --> 00:06:45.336
The leverage trap.

00:06:46.636 --> 00:06:49.554
Here's exactly how this discount turns into

00:06:49.554 --> 00:06:53.069
forced liquidation, specifically for anyone using an

00:06:53.069 --> 00:06:56.221
L.S.T. as collateral, close to their limit.

00:06:56.221 --> 00:07:00.472
A protocol's own oracle reads the L.S.T.'s current market

00:07:00.472 --> 00:07:05.316
price, discount included, not its underlying redemption value.

00:07:05.316 --> 00:07:08.771
If that discounted price crosses your collateral's own

00:07:08.771 --> 00:07:13.106
liquidation threshold, you're liquidated immediately, at exactly the

00:07:13.106 --> 00:07:16.184
wrong moment, even though the asset itself never

00:07:16.184 --> 00:07:18.446
actually lost any real value at all.

00:07:19.746 --> 00:07:23.396
Here's that exact same two percent discount, applied to

00:07:23.396 --> 00:07:27.503
two different borrowers against the same L.S.T. collateral, to

00:07:27.503 --> 00:07:30.671
make the rule concrete rather than abstract.

00:07:30.671 --> 00:07:32.706
A borrower with real room to spare

00:07:32.706 --> 00:07:35.323
simply absorbs that two percent inside their

00:07:35.323 --> 00:07:38.181
existing buffer, and nothing happens at all.

00:07:38.181 --> 00:07:40.620
A borrower already sitting right at their

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:42.885
limit gets liquidated by that same two

00:07:42.885 --> 00:07:45.673
percent, purely because they left themselves no

00:07:45.673 --> 00:07:47.589
buffer for it in the first place.

00:07:48.889 --> 00:07:52.120
Here's the actual rule this entire chain of reasoning

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:55.052
leads to, and it's worth remembering on its own,

00:07:55.052 --> 00:07:58.104
independent of everything else in this lesson.

00:07:58.104 --> 00:08:00.859
Never borrow so close to your limit that

00:08:00.859 --> 00:08:04.756
an L.S.T. discount alone, entirely temporary and entirely

00:08:04.756 --> 00:08:08.183
disconnected from the asset's real value, could be

00:08:08.183 --> 00:08:10.199
the thing that liquidates you.

00:08:11.499 --> 00:08:12.869
Checking the queue yourself.

00:08:14.169 --> 00:08:17.147
Here's how to actually check a redemption queue before

00:08:17.147 --> 00:08:19.504
you rely on being able to wait it out.

00:08:19.504 --> 00:08:23.031
Check the protocol's own documentation or dashboard for

00:08:23.031 --> 00:08:26.354
its current stated unbonding or redemption time.

00:08:26.354 --> 00:08:28.432
Check whether that time is fixed, or

00:08:28.432 --> 00:08:30.622
can extend under heavy demand, since a

00:08:30.622 --> 00:08:32.944
rush of withdrawals can lengthen it.

00:08:32.944 --> 00:08:35.721
And size your own discount tolerance around that

00:08:35.721 --> 00:08:38.384
real number, not around an assumption that you

00:08:38.384 --> 00:08:40.424
can always exit whenever you'd like.

00:08:41.724 --> 00:08:43.184
Here's your checklist.

00:08:43.184 --> 00:08:47.369
Do it before using any L.S.T., especially as collateral.

00:08:47.369 --> 00:08:50.948
Know its accrual method, rebasing or exchange rate, since

00:08:50.948 --> 00:08:54.149
that changes how it behaves in other contracts.

00:08:54.149 --> 00:08:56.626
Know its redemption path, and specifically

00:08:56.626 --> 00:08:58.884
how long that queue actually runs.

00:08:58.884 --> 00:09:01.380
And set a discount alert for yourself, if

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:04.054
you're using it as collateral, so a widening

00:09:04.054 --> 00:09:06.074
gap never catches you by surprise.

00:09:07.374 --> 00:09:08.619
Let's recap.

00:09:08.619 --> 00:09:11.974
An L.S.T. represents staked assets plus accrued

00:09:11.974 --> 00:09:15.769
rewards, still tradeable and usable as collateral.

00:09:15.769 --> 00:09:18.234
It accrues value either by rebasing your

00:09:18.234 --> 00:09:21.120
balance, or through a rising exchange rate.

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:23.507
Under real stress, it can trade at a

00:09:23.507 --> 00:09:26.410
discount to its redemption value, a gap that

00:09:26.410 --> 00:09:29.548
only becomes a real loss for forced sellers.

00:09:29.548 --> 00:09:31.801
And the rule that follows from all of

00:09:31.801 --> 00:09:34.113
it: never borrow so close to the limit

00:09:34.113 --> 00:09:37.078
that a temporary discount alone can liquidate you.

00:09:38.378 --> 00:09:41.504
Know your LST's accrual method and redemption queue,

00:09:41.504 --> 00:09:43.804
and never borrow so close to the limit

00:09:43.804 --> 00:09:46.463
that a discount alone can liquidate you.

00:09:46.463 --> 00:09:50.338
Next up, Lesson 4.4: restaking and shared security.
